A state machine is a JSON document with StartAt + States:
{
"Comment": "minimal example",
"StartAt": "Greet",
"States": {
"Greet": {
"Type": "Task",
"Resource": "arn:aws:lambda:ap-east-1:123456789012:function:hello",
"End": true
}
}
}
Run:
aws stepfunctions start-execution \
--state-machine-arn arn:aws:states:... \
--input '{"name":"Ada"}'
Every state has a Type. The major ones follow.

Invokes an AWS service action. The most common is a Lambda, but Step Functions can call > 200 AWS service actions directly without a Lambda:
"WriteToDdb": {
"Type": "Task",
"Resource": "arn:aws:states:::dynamodb:putItem",
"Parameters": {
"TableName": "Orders",
"Item": {
"order_id": { "S.$": "$.order_id" },
"status": { "S": "received" }
}
},
"Next": "ChargeCard"
}
The .$ suffix tells the engine "use the value from the execution state at this JSONPath". This service integration avoids a Lambda hop entirely — faster + cheaper + simpler.

Conditional branching.
"CheckPriority": {
"Type": "Choice",
"Choices": [
{ "Variable": "$.priority", "StringEquals": "high", "Next": "RushDeliver" },
{ "Variable": "$.priority", "StringEquals": "low", "Next": "Queue" }
],
"Default": "Standard"
}

Pauses for a duration or until a timestamp.
"WaitADay": {
"Type": "Wait",
"Seconds": 86400,
"Next": "FollowUp"
}

Runs multiple branches at once; collects all results into an array.
"FanOut": {
"Type": "Parallel",
"Branches": [
{ "StartAt": "FetchPricing", "States": { "FetchPricing": { "Type": "Task", "Resource": "...", "End": true } } },
{ "StartAt": "FetchInventory", "States": { "FetchInventory": { "Type": "Task", "Resource": "...", "End": true } } }
],
"Next": "Combine"
}

Like Parallel, but for a dynamic-size array — the same workflow runs once per item.
"ProcessAll": {
"Type": "Map",
"ItemsPath": "$.orders",
"MaxConcurrency": 10,
"Iterator": {
"StartAt": "ProcessOne",
"States": { "ProcessOne": { "Type": "Task", "Resource": "...", "End": true } }
},
"End": true
}
The 2023+ Distributed Map scales Map up to 10,000 parallel iterations (good for fanning out over big S3 lists, large arrays).

Every Task can declare retries and error catchers:
"ChargeCard": {
"Type": "Task",
"Resource": "arn:aws:lambda:...:function:charge",
"Retry": [
{
"ErrorEquals": ["PaymentService.Timeout"],
"IntervalSeconds": 2,
"BackoffRate": 2.0,
"MaxAttempts": 3
},
{
"ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
"IntervalSeconds": 1,
"MaxAttempts": 1
}
],
"Catch": [
{
"ErrorEquals": ["PaymentService.Declined"],
"Next": "NotifyDeclined"
},
{
"ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
"Next": "Rollback"
}
],
"Next": "ShipOrder"
}
Retries multiply: first the most specific error block runs, then States.ALL as a catch-all.
Trick: put exponential backoff (BackoffRate: 2.0) on Retries that hit external APIs — the third-party will recover; instant retries waste your throttling budget.

Workflow: user uploads photo → resize multiple variants in parallel → run ML classifier → store metadata → notify user.
{
"Comment": "Image pipeline",
"StartAt": "FanOutResize",
"States": {
"FanOutResize": {
"Type": "Parallel",
"Branches": [
{ "StartAt": "Thumb", "States": { "Thumb": { "Type": "Task", "Resource": "...:resize-thumb", "End": true } } },
{ "StartAt": "Medium", "States": { "Medium": { "Type": "Task", "Resource": "...:resize-medium", "End": true } } },
{ "StartAt": "Large", "States": { "Large": { "Type": "Task", "Resource": "...:resize-large", "End": true } } }
],
"Next": "Classify"
},
"Classify": {
"Type": "Task",
"Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
"Parameters": {
"FunctionName": "ml-classifier",
"Payload.$": "$"
},
"Retry": [{ "ErrorEquals": ["Lambda.TooManyRequestsException"], "IntervalSeconds": 1, "MaxAttempts": 3 }],
"Next": "StoreMeta"
},
"StoreMeta": {
"Type": "Task",
"Resource": "arn:aws:states:::dynamodb:putItem",
"Parameters": {
"TableName": "Images",
"Item": {
"image_id": { "S.$": "$.image_id" },
"classified": { "BOOL": true }
}
},
"Next": "Notify"
},
"Notify": {
"Type": "Task",
"Resource": "arn:aws:states:::sns:publish",
"Parameters": {
"TopicArn": "arn:aws:sns:...:image-ready",
"Message.$": "$.image_id"
},
"End": true
}
}
}
What this gains over hand-wired Lambdas:
Visual diagram for every execution.
Built-in retries with backoff.
Direct SDK integration for DynamoDB + SNS — no Lambda wrappers.
Full execution history queryable in the console.
Easy modification — add a step in JSON, no code change.

A common pattern: wait for a human approval mid-workflow. Step Functions supports callback tokens — pause until an out-of-band call resumes.
"AwaitApproval": {
"Type": "Task",
"Resource": "arn:aws:states:::sqs:sendMessage.waitForTaskToken",
"Parameters": {
"QueueUrl": "https://sqs...",
"MessageBody": {
"approval_url": "https://my-app/approve",
"token.$": "$$.Task.Token"
}
},
"Next": "Continue"
}
The workflow pauses (up to a year on Standard). A human (or webhook) calls SendTaskSuccess / SendTaskFailure with the token to resume.
